  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure will remain centered over Quebec heading \nthrough tomorrow, keeping the weather mostly dry. Increasing low-\nlevel flow will help keep the boundary layer a bit coupled tonight \nand prevent much fog or frost formation. A few showers are possible \nover southern Vermont late tomorrow, while central and northern \nareas will remain dry with only some high clouds. The nor'easter \nwill remain centered off the Mid-Atlantic Coast heading through \nSaturday night and Sunday. Its stationary position will help \nmoisture wrap around to the west, and multiple bands of rain \nwill move across southern Vermont and attempt to make their way \nnorth. However, they will be displaced far from the center of \nthe low and will be heading into very dry air so by the time \nthey reach northern areas, they will mostly fall apart. \nTherefore, while southern Vermont will see multiple rounds of \nshowers, northern areas will only see a few sprinkles and mostly\ndry weather. As the system occludes and a trough pushes in \ncloser from the northwest on Monday and Tuesday, the nor'easter \nwill reenter the background flow and get pulled northeast and \nout of the region. As it moves north, multiple rounds of showers\nare possible even in northern areas Monday into Monday night. \n\nSome gusty winds will occur late Saturday into Sunday as the low-\nlevel jet moves overhead, particularly in southern Vermont. It \nshould have winds in the 35-50 KT at around summit level, and 30-40 \nKT looks to be under the inversion level. This will likely cause \nincreased gusts along the western slopes due to downsloping, but \neven if conditions become very favorable, the strength of the low-\nlevel jet will limit gusts. Therefore, gusts in the 30-40 mph range \nare possible along the western slopes of the southern Greens.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure moves in Tuesday as our nor'easter moves \nout to sea. We'll have a warming trend through Thursday, with \ntemperatures reaching into the mid-70s in the Champlain Valley. A \ncold front will come through later in the day Thursday bringing some \nrain and dropping our temperatures back into the 60s for most \nlocations.",
